# beta-tester credentials — SCHEMA ONLY, blank values, safe to commit.
#
# The real file lives with the project under test, in its git directory. The skill resolves its
# path from the current directory; the first rule that applies wins, and a file missing where it
# points is an error, never a reason to try the next rule:
#
#     $BETA_TESTER_ENV                  an override, when set
#     <git common dir>/beta-tester.env  inside a git repo: .git/beta-tester.env, shared by every
#                                       linked worktree
#     $HOME/.beta-tester.env            only outside any git repo
#
# chmod 0600 — the skill refuses to run if any permission bit is set for group or others.
#
# git never versions anything inside .git, so the file can't be committed or pushed, and a branch
# switch or `git clean` can't touch it. Accounts are per project: inside a repo, a file in $HOME is
# not consulted. It is still inside the checkout, though: make sure no web server, dev server or
# image build exposes .git (see references/accounts-and-credentials.md), or keep the file outside
# and point $BETA_TESTER_ENV at it.
#
# Copy this schema to the resolved path and fill in real values. Never commit the real file. The
# skill reads it only through scripts/beta-env.sh, which parses it and never executes it, and it
# keeps _PASSWORD and _API_TOKEN values out of reports and filed tasks. Use test accounts only: a
# value typed into a browser or passed to a tool still ends up in the session transcript.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Schema
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#     BETA_TESTER_<PERSONA>_EMAIL
#     BETA_TESTER_<PERSONA>_PASSWORD
#     BETA_TESTER_<PERSONA>_API_TOKEN    optional, enables token-based API calls for this persona
#     BETA_TESTER_<PERSONA>_LABEL        optional, one line, shown in the account picker
#     BETA_TESTER_<PERSONA>_TYPED_LOGIN  optional, "allow": the skill may type this persona's
#                                        password into the browser, or pass its credentials to an
#                                        MCP tool, without asking each run. The value then lands
#                                        in the session transcript, so set it only on throwaway
#                                        test accounts.
#
# <PERSONA>  a free identifier: DEFAULT, ADMIN, TRIAL, PAID, whatever the account is for in this
#            project.
#
# Personas are discovered by scanning key names, so adding an account here is the only step. The
# scan (beta_personas in scripts/beta-env.sh) marks a persona UI when _EMAIL and _PASSWORD are
# both set and API when _API_TOKEN is, so a persona may be UI-only, API-only, or both. It accepts
# `export` lines, and it treats a blank or whitespace-only value, or a leftover placeholder
# (change-me, an @example.com address), as not set, so an unfilled persona is never offered in
# the picker.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# _API_TOKEN's shape
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Auth shapes vary by target: a bearer header, a custom header pair, a query parameter, Basic
# auth. This file holds one credential value per persona; the skill asks once, before the first
# API-mode request of a run, how the target actually wants it sent, and remembers the answer for
# the rest of the run. Get the token however the target's own account/API-key settings issue one.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why more than one account
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Many products gate behaviour per account — a plan tier, a role, a feature flag. Testing a gated
# change as a plain account often doesn't fail loudly: it just exercises core behaviour and
# reports a clean pass. Configure one account per state you need to cover, and let the skill ask
# which to use. Authorization probes also need a second, unrelated account to play the other
# caller.

# --- example accounts — fill in the blanks ---------------------------------------------------

BETA_TESTER_DEFAULT_EMAIL=
BETA_TESTER_DEFAULT_PASSWORD=
BETA_TESTER_DEFAULT_API_TOKEN=
BETA_TESTER_DEFAULT_LABEL=Plain account, no special entitlements

BETA_TESTER_ADMIN_EMAIL=
BETA_TESTER_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
BETA_TESTER_ADMIN_LABEL=Admin-role account — UI only, no API token

BETA_TESTER_PAID_EMAIL=
BETA_TESTER_PAID_PASSWORD=
BETA_TESTER_PAID_API_TOKEN=
BETA_TESTER_PAID_LABEL=Paid-tier account with the gated feature set

BETA_TESTER_OTHER_EMAIL=
BETA_TESTER_OTHER_PASSWORD=
BETA_TESTER_OTHER_API_TOKEN=
BETA_TESTER_OTHER_LABEL=Unrelated account, the second caller for authorization probes
# BETA_TESTER_OTHER_TYPED_LOGIN=allow
